{"name":"Salt Cellar","hex":"#dee0d7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"salt cellar","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small open container holding salt for use in the kitchen or on a dining table.","Synonym of salt shaker (“a closed container with perforated lid for sprinkling salt”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salt_cellar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salt cellar","blocks":[{"text":"A salt cellar is an article of tableware for holding and dispensing salt. In British English, the term can be used for what in North American English are called salt shakers. Salt cellars can be either lidded or open, and are found in a wide range of sizes, from large shared vessels to small individual dishes. Styles range from simple to ornate or whimsical, using materials including glass and ceramic, metals, ivory and wood, and plastic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Glass_salt_cellar_1720.jpg/330px-Glass_salt_cellar_1720.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_cellar","wikidata":"Q5771190"},{"title":"Salt","blocks":[{"text":"Salt is a mineral composed primarily of sodium chloride (NaCl). When used in food, especially in granulated form, it is more formally called table salt. In the form of a natural crystalline mineral, salt is also known as rock salt or halite. Salt is essential for life in general, and saltiness is one of the basic human tastes. Salt is one of the oldest and most ubiquitous food seasonings, and is known to uniformly improve the taste perception of food. Salting, brining, and pickling are ancient and important methods of food preservation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg/330px-Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt","wikidata":"Q11254"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Salt_cellar_Athens_Louvre_ED524.jpg/500px-Salt_cellar_Athens_Louvre_ED524.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Salt_cellar_Athens_Louvre_ED524.jpg/1280px-Salt_cellar_Athens_Louvre_ED524.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Salt_cellar_Athens_Louvre_ED524.jpg/500px-Salt_cellar_Athens_Louvre_ED524.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black-glaze salt cellar. Terracotta, 5th century BC, Athens.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalt_cellar_Athens_Louvre_ED524.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Salt_Cellar.jpg/500px-Salt_Cellar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Salt_Cellar.jpg/1280px-Salt_Cellar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Salt_Cellar.jpg/500px-Salt_Cellar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cellini's Salt Cellar, made for Francis I of France, 1540–1543. Gold, partly enameled, with an ebony base. Depicts Earth and Sea personified.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalt_Cellar.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Formal_Place_Setting_12_Course_Dinner.jpg/500px-Formal_Place_Setting_12_Course_Dinner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_